herefore,\n\n$$\n1[0\\le 3]=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521478 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521425 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521478 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346659" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521478 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521478 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the indicator function\n$1[9\\in \\{1,2,3,5\\}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521478 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "30559f45-859e-514a-9084-78b866aea583" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521478 #edb/ref 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the predicate is $9\\in \\{1,2,3,5\\}.$ Since $9$ is not an element of the set $\\{1,2,3,5\\},$ the predicate is false. Therefore,\n\n$$\n1[9\\in \\{1,2,3,5\\}]=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521491 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521351 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521491 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346661" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521491 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521491 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $B=\\{2,4,6,8\\}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${1}_{B}(4)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521491 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "45172067-6fd8-53b2-8b3e-3767eb3b99d5" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521491 #edb/ref 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{B}(x)$ of a set $B$ equals $1$ if $x\\in B$ and $0$ if $x\\notin B.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{B}(x)=1[x\\in B].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{1}_{B}(4)=1[4\\in B]=1[4\\in \\{2,4,6,8\\}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521492 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521413 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521492 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346664" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521492 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521492 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $E=\\{1,4,9\\}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to ${1}_{E}(9)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521492 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "4946e8ca-eef4-5bca-ab77-2e318993cb3a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521492 #edb/ref 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{E}(x)$ of a set $E$ equals $1$ if $x\\in E$ and $0$ if $x\\notin E.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{E}(x)=1[x\\in E].\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n{1}_{E}(9)=1[9\\in E]=1[9\\in \\{1,4,9\\}].\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521512 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521445 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521512 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346656" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521512 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521512 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the indicator function\n$1[3\\in \\{1,3,6,8\\}].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521512 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "77a3c45c-df66-5438-9ce6-8791cf4f16da" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521512 #edb/ref 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the predicate is $3\\in \\{1,3,6,8\\}.$ Since $3$ is an element of the set $\\{1,3,6,8\\},$ the predicate is true. Therefore,\n\n$$\n1[3\\in \\{1,3,6,8\\}]=1.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521515 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521388 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521515 #edb/ref 1069 "q-347932" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521515 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521515 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{x}_{1}=-3,\\,{x}_{2}=1,\\,{x}_{3}=6,\\,{x}_{4}=-2,\\,{x}_{5}=0\n$$\n\nGiven the values ${x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4},\\,{x}_{5}$ above, we have\n\n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot ({x}_{i}-1)+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 2=$ {{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521515 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "7ce60c02-eb25-536f-a376-c9d5685f21ea" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521515 #edb/ref 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression $1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot ({x}_{i}-1)+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 2$ equals ${x}_{i}-1$ when ${x}_{i}\\ge 0$ and equals $2$ when ${x}_{i}<0.$ Thus, the sum\n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot ({x}_{i}-1)+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 2\n$$\n\nadds ${x}_{i}-1$ for nonnegative values and $2$ for negative values.\n\nWe evaluate the indicator function for each ${x}_{i}:$\n\n- $1[{x}_{1}\\ge 0]=1[-3\\ge 0]=0,$ $1[{x}_{1}<0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{2}\\ge 0]=1[1\\ge 0]=1,$ $1[{x}_{2}<0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{3}\\ge 0]=1[6\\ge 0]=1,$ $1[{x}_{3}<0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{4}\\ge 0]=1[-2\\ge 0]=0,$ $1[{x}_{4}<0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{5}\\ge 0]=1[0\\ge 0]=1,$ $1[{x}_{5}<0]=0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}\\ge 0]\\cdot ({x}_{i}-1)+1[{x}_{i}<0]\\cdot 2 & =2+(1-1)+(6-1)+2+(0-1) \\\\  & =2+0+5+2-1 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521540 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521428 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521540 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346652" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521540 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521540 #edb/ref 1071 "Evaluate the indicator function\n$1[9<3].$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521540 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "b1a403c5-0788-5593-a233-1f2c8a521c2c" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521540 #edb/ref 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nHere, the predicate is $9<3.$ Since $9$ is not less than $3,$ the predicate is false. Therefore,\n\n$$\n1[9<3]=0.\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521549 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521401 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521549 #edb/ref 1069 "q-347433" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521549 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521549 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function\n\n$$\ng(x)=1[x\\ge 2]\\cdot (x-2)^{2}+1[-1\\le x<2]\\cdot 1+1[x<-1]\\cdot (-x).\n$$\n\nWhat is $g(-3)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521549 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "cde00f71-de2d-5ff7-ae71-1ddcfa0a1b1f" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521549 #edb/ref 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3,$ we evaluate each indicator function:\n\n- $1[x\\ge 2]=1[-3\\ge 2]=0$ (since $-3\\ge 2$ is false)\n\n- $1[-1\\le x<2]=1[-1\\le -3<2]=0$ (since $-1\\le -3$ is false)\n\n- $1[x<-1]=1[-3<-1]=1$ (since $-3<-1$ is true)\n\nSubstituting into the expression for $g(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}g(-3) & =1[-3\\ge 2]\\cdot (-5)^{2}+1[-1\\le -3<2]\\cdot 1+1[-3<-1]\\cdot (-(-3)) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 25+0\\cdot 1+1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the indicator functions act as a \"switch\": since $x=-3<-1,$ only the third term contributes to the result." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521550 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521363 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521550 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346669" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521550 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521550 #edb/ref 1071 "$$\n{x}_{1}=-3,\\,{x}_{2}=6,\\,{x}_{3}=0,\\,{x}_{4}=2,\\,{x}_{5}=-1\n$$\n\nGiven the values ${x}_{1},\\,{x}_{2},\\,{x}_{3},\\,{x}_{4},\\,{x}_{5}$ above, we have\n\n${\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}=$ {{field-1}}." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521550 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "ce2c0db9-ad0b-5196-91d2-2f4a888aa062" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521550 #edb/ref 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe expression $1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}$ equals ${x}_{i}$ when ${x}_{i}>0$ and equals $0$ otherwise. Thus, the sum \n\n$$\n{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i}\n$$\n\nadds up only the positive values.\n\nWe evaluate the indicator function for each ${x}_{i}:$\n\n- $1[{x}_{1}>0]=1[-3>0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{2}>0]=1[6>0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{3}>0]=1[0>0]=0$\n\n- $1[{x}_{4}>0]=1[2>0]=1$\n\n- $1[{x}_{5}>0]=1[-1>0]=0$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{i=1}^{5}1[{x}_{i}>0]\\cdot {x}_{i} & =0\\cdot (-3)+1\\cdot 6+0\\cdot 0+1\\cdot 2+0\\cdot (-1) \\\\  & =0+6+0+2+0 \\\\  & =8.\\end{aligned}\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521551 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521360 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521551 #edb/ref 1069 "q-351127" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521551 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521551 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $C=\\{1,3,7,9\\}.$ Which of the following is equivalent to $1[4\\in C]?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521551 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "d217e754-13bb-55c1-bf52-1609c0455dd0" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521551 #edb/ref 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nRecall that the indicator function ${1}_{A}(x)$ of a set $A$ equals $1$ if $x\\in A$ and $0$ if $x\\notin A.$ This can be written using Iverson bracket notation as\n\n$$\n{1}_{A}(x)=1[x\\in A].\n$$\n\nHere, the Iverson bracket expression $1[4\\in C]$ has $x=4$ and $A=C.$ Therefore, converting back to set indicator notation, we have\n\n$$\n1[4\\in C]={1}_{C}(4).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521559 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521334 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521559 #edb/ref 1069 "q-346680" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521559 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045487 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521559 #edb/ref 1071 "Consider the function\n\n$$\nf(x)=1[x\\ge -1]\\cdot {x}^{2}+1[x<-1]\\cdot (-x).\n$$\n\nWhat is $f(-3)?$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521559 #edb/ref 1072 #uuid "e022df96-ab83-5f54-81a6-300069e7bfc3" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521559 #edb/ref 1074 "The indicator function $1[P]$ equals $1$ if the predicate $P$ is true and $0$ if $P$ is false.\n\n$$\n1[P]=\\{\\begin{aligned}1, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is true}, \\\\ 0, & \\text{if }P\\text{ is false}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFor $x=-3,$ we evaluate each indicator function:\n\n- $1[x\\ge -1]=1[-3\\ge -1]=0$ (since $-3\\ge -1$ is false)\n\n- $1[x<-1]=1[-3<-1]=1$ (since $-3<-1$ is true)\n\nSubstituting into the expression for $f(x),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(-3) & =1[-3\\ge -1]\\cdot (-3)^{2}+1[-3<-1]\\cdot (-(-3)) \\\\  & =0\\cdot 9+1\\cdot 3 \\\\  & =3.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote that the indicator functions act as a \"switch\": since $x=-3<-1,$ the first term vanishes and only the second term contributes to the result." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521564 #edb/ref 1068 #edb/ref 17592186521370 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521564 #edb/ref 1069 "q-351130" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521564 #edb/ref 1070 #edb/ref 17592186045485 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186521564 #edb/ref 1071 "Let $F=\\{3,6,9,15\\}.$ Which of the following is equivalent 